    cold-4 step 2 {"follow":"drove__held_rhyne"}: lines: "A quay of grey stone runs out into the water at Porthalen, its edge worn round and green with weed, and the town climbs behind it. Gulls sit along it in a row, facing the wind, over the slap of water on stone. The Whitewater runs by below the houses. Twice this month the Hall’s keeper has driven the town’s pigs off the verge of the old road. The geese are kept in a bunch at the well, hissing at the lane to the Hall, and three hens have gone from the inn’s yard since Tuesday." -> "A quay of grey stone runs out into the water at Porthalen, its edge worn round and green with weed, and the town climbs behind it. Gulls sit along it in a row, facing the wind, over the slap of water on stone. The Whitewater runs by below the houses. The boat-sheds are short of oak this year: the Hall has chained the road-wood, and the good timber is behind it. At dusk every dog in the town turns its head toward the hill, and the geese go silent."
